WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.934
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.934 --> 00:00:03.103
is a United States Senator
from Vermont.

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:04.270
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.038
our friend
Senator Bernie Sanders.

00:00:06.038 --> 00:00:07.674
Hello, Senator!

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.208
-How are you, Seth?
-Good.

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.177
You know, we last spoke
right before the election,

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:15.014
and now here we are in the
first full day of the Biden era.

00:00:15.014 --> 00:00:16.616
How are you feeling?

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:21.788
-I am feeling great.
I -- I was almost in te--

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:24.157
I was at
the inauguration yesterday,

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:28.228
and I was in tears
seeing the new President

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:33.433
getting sworn in and the old
president leaving Washington.

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.702
-It will not surprise you
to know that I was aware

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:37.803
you were at the inauguration.

00:00:37.803 --> 00:00:40.240
[ Both laugh ]

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.408
-I heard about that.
-Yeah.

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:47.380
I would say, you know,
outside of Amanda,

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.515
the inaugural poet
who was incredible,

00:00:49.515 --> 00:00:52.518
you were sort of one of
the stars of the inauguration.

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:56.622
Were you aware that this photo
was immediately --

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:59.225
sort of became
the meme of the day?

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.727
-Not at all.
I was just sitting there,

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:03.028
trying to keep warm,

00:01:03.028 --> 00:01:06.098
trying to pay attention
to what was going on.

00:01:06.098 --> 00:01:08.801
-I appreciate that you're
not a politician who uses memes,

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:11.837
but you became one.
I mean, do you --

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:13.439
Does your staff
show them to you?

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:16.509
Here you are with
the "Sex and the City" girls.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.511
-[ Laughs ]
Yeah, I've seen them.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.946
I've seen them.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.946 --> 00:01:22.915
-The other question --
-What was really nice, Seth,

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:25.484
is the woman
who made the mittens

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:27.920
lives in Essex Junction,
Vermont.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.924
She is a schoolteacher.
And a very, very nice person.

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:35.127
And she has been
somewhat overwhelmed

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:38.697
by the kind of attention that
is being shown to her mittens.

00:01:38.697 --> 00:01:41.267
-You know, she struck me
as the kind of supporter

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.069
who would make
Bernie Sanders mittens

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:45.738
because I read her saying that a
lot of people have reached out

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:47.573
asking for the mittens
and she basically said,

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:50.043
"Look, I work.
I'm a mother.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.844
I don't have time
to make you mittens."

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:53.912
And I thought
that is very in line

00:01:53.912 --> 00:01:57.416
with what I expect of you,
as well -- all business.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.451
And I promise --
I promise to get

00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:01.186
to a substantive
discussion here.

00:02:01.186 --> 00:02:02.989
I don't want to waste all
the time talking about memes.

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:04.490
But the other question,
of course, is,

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:05.958
people were asking
what you were carrying.

00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:08.561
What was your manila envelope?

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.796
-I'd love to tell you, Seth.
It's top-secret.

00:02:10.796 --> 00:02:12.565
[ Laughter ]
-You know what?

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:17.003
I appreciate it.
I appreciate your honesty.

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:18.670
You know,
I think one of the things

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.674
we've talked about in the past
is that you obviously

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.309
are more progressive
than the President,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:26.345
but you're
a longtime friend of his.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:32.118
Are you encouraged thus far
by how you're collaborating,

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:34.220
how you're working together?

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.556
-I think the President
is off to a very good start.

00:02:37.556 --> 00:02:40.093
And what I liked about
his inaugural remarks

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:44.097
and the general tone
of his administration

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:47.099
is he recognizes
that this country faces

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:51.537
a set of unprecedented crises,
from the pandemic

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:55.140
to the economy, to the very
threats against democracy,

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.977
to climate change,
to income and wealth inequality.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.878
I think he understands that.

00:02:59.878 --> 00:03:02.448
And I think if we can give him
the support that he needs

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:05.051
in Congress, he can, in fact,

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:07.453
be an extremely
progressive president

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:11.423
addressing the crises
facing working families.

00:03:11.423 --> 00:03:14.560
-You've talked in the past about
reaching out to Trump voters

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.099
who felt they were left behind
by this economy.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:22.335
What we saw on January 6th,
I think it's becoming

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:24.236
more and more clear
those were not people

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:28.440
that were driven by economic
anxiety who stormed the Capitol.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.410
Is there a way to,
as a Democratic Party,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.914
reach out to those people,
or do you have to just accept

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.183
there is never going to be
middle ground with people

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.652
who make choices like that?

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.187
-I think
the latter point is correct.

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:43.423
I mean,
those are fanatical people

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:46.225
who do not believe in democracy,

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:50.062
who are in many,
many cases white supremacists.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:52.565
I'm not quite sure
how you can reach out to them.

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:56.869
But I think that there are
tens of millions of other people

00:03:56.869 --> 00:04:00.907
who voted for Trump
who are hurting economically,

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:03.843
who are worried about
the future for their kids,

00:04:03.843 --> 00:04:06.512
who are worried
about the opioid epidemic

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.748
that is hitting
their communities,

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.616
who are worried
that they do not have

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:12.684
the kind of
decent-paying jobs they need,

00:04:12.684 --> 00:04:13.986
the healthcare they need,

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:16.789
the educational opportunities
their family needs.

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:20.659
And I think we can
reach out to those people.

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:22.995
You know,
what Trump tried to do,

00:04:22.995 --> 00:04:26.064
spent four years
trying to divide us up

00:04:26.064 --> 00:04:29.769
based on the color of our skin,
where we were born,

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:32.872
our sexual orientation,
our religion.

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:36.075
And our job is to try
to do the best we can

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:39.145
in bringing people together

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:42.481
around an agenda
that works for all.

00:04:42.481 --> 00:04:45.384
Whether you are a conservative
living in rural America

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.053
or a progressive
in New York City,

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.587
you need decent healthcare.

00:04:48.587 --> 00:04:51.123
That's what you need.
You need a decent-paying job.

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:53.292
So I want to focus
on those issues

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.726
that bring people together

00:04:54.726 --> 00:04:58.730
and that improves the quality
of life for Americans.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:01.767
-President Biden made, I think,
a great effort yesterday

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:05.571
to send a message of unity,
and he is someone who talked

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.773
in the lead-up to the election
about how he is

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.408
the kind of politician
who has friends

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:10.910
on the other side of the aisle.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.445
He wants
to work with Republicans.

00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:16.282
Yet we're already seeing
even today that Mitch McConnell

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:18.317
is taking steps to limit

00:05:18.317 --> 00:05:21.654
what the Democrats
in the Senate can do.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:25.457
Do you think there is some sort
of bipartisan work

00:05:25.457 --> 00:05:26.893
that can be done in the Senate,

00:05:26.893 --> 00:05:31.364
or are you expecting a sort of
McConnell-inspired gridlock?

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:35.201
-Look, I think
we should do our best

00:05:35.201 --> 00:05:38.304
to reach out to Republicans

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:42.208
who represent communities
that are suffering terribly,

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:46.779
in terms of unemployment,
lack of healthcare,

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:48.881
and other very serious problems.

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:53.886
But I don't think our reaching
out should go on indefinitely.

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.689
This country today is hurting,

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:58.824
and people are hurting
really, really badly.

00:05:58.824 --> 00:06:00.426
We're looking at people,

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:03.262
people who cannot
feed their kids, literally,

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:04.664
who are worried about
being evicted,

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:07.566
can't afford to go to the doctor
even when they are sick.

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:09.402
We have got to move
and move quickly.

00:06:09.402 --> 00:06:11.637
So I think we should
reach out to Republicans.

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:14.106
If they choose not
to come on board,

00:06:14.106 --> 00:06:16.809
which I suspect
will probably be the case,

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:20.078
we have the majority,
we should use that majority

00:06:20.078 --> 00:06:21.313
in a very aggressive way.

00:06:21.313 --> 00:06:25.084
Now, I'm gonna be chairman
of the Budget Committee,

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:28.554
which handles what we call
reconciliation,

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:31.524
and that is a Senate process
by which you can pass

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:33.859
not all kinds of legislation

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:36.629
but a whole lot
of very important legislation

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:40.032
with a majority vote,
not 60 votes.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:44.036
And it is my view we should
make sure that we address

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:47.706
the needs of the American people
in that reconciliation bill,

00:06:47.706 --> 00:06:52.578
and if we pass it with 51 votes,
we'll pass it with 51 votes.

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:55.747
-One of the questions that is
being asked of a lot of people

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:57.717
in the Senate right now,
especially with all

00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:01.120
that President Biden
wants to accomplish quickly

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.456
due to the fact
that we are facing

00:07:03.456 --> 00:07:07.826
the multitude of problems
you mentioned, is do we also --

00:07:07.826 --> 00:07:11.730
or, should I say, will
impeachment be a distraction?

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:14.300
Do you think, moving forward,
that, one,

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.002
the Senate can balance
both getting things done

00:07:17.002 --> 00:07:19.672
and an impeachment,
and, two, is the impeachment,

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:23.375
in your mind, an important thing
to move forward with?

00:07:23.375 --> 00:07:26.078
-Well, I think both.
I mean, I think it will be

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:28.147
an impediment to some degree,
but I think it's something

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:31.117
that has to be done.
And I'll tell you why, Seth.

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:36.321
History must record
that this Congress made it clear

00:07:36.321 --> 00:07:42.894
that no President can incite
his followers into insurrection,

00:07:42.894 --> 00:07:45.130
that that is an outrageous act.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:48.434
That is an attack on
the Democratic foundations

00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:53.639
on which this country is built,
and he must be impeached.

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:56.709
Having said that,
there are so many problems

00:07:56.709 --> 00:07:58.544
facing this country
that I want to see

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:00.846
this impeachment process
move forward.

00:08:00.846 --> 00:08:04.383
I want to see him convicted
as quickly as possible.

00:08:04.383 --> 00:08:06.585
We have got to show
the American people

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:10.122
that we can walk and chew
bubble gum at the same time.

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:13.592
We don't have the time to spend
weeks and months on impeachment.

00:08:13.592 --> 00:08:15.827
People are hungry
in America today.

00:08:15.827 --> 00:08:19.030
They're facing eviction.
They have no income.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:20.298
They're worried
about the future.

00:08:20.298 --> 00:08:22.034
We've got climate change
out there.

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:24.803
We got 90 million people
uninsured or underinsured.

00:08:24.803 --> 00:08:28.374
We have got to move aggressively
to protect the American people,

00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:32.444
and we can go forward
simultaneously

00:08:32.444 --> 00:08:34.012
on all of these issues

00:08:34.012 --> 00:08:37.216
if we're prepared to roll up
our sleeves and get to work.

00:08:37.216 --> 00:08:39.418
-Well, I really appreciate
you being here.

00:08:39.418 --> 00:08:42.254
I do want to close with my --
I think this is my favorite one

00:08:42.254 --> 00:08:44.122
because I just don't think
you would listen

00:08:44.122 --> 00:08:45.324
to Forrest Gump that long.

00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:47.493
I think you would get up
and leave that bench.

00:08:47.493 --> 00:08:51.597
[ Both laugh ]
Hey, thank you, so much.

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:53.399
We'll see you soon.
I really appreciate it.

